reasoning:
remap_3d_kokkos() (remap_kokkos.cpp:105-...) contains, unconditionally on every call when GPU-aware MPI is off:
  if (!plan->usegpu_aware) {
    plan->h_scratch = Kokkos::create_mirror_view(d_scratch);
    plan->h_sendbuf = Kokkos::create_mirror_view(plan->d_sendbuf);
(lines 123-127). On a GPU build the mirror space differs from the device space, so create_mirror_view allocates a fresh host View each time and the assignment drops the previous one -- a malloc/free pair of the full scratch and sendbuf size per remap. h_scratch and h_sendbuf are already persistent plan members (remap_kokkos.h:33,35) and their device counterparts are allocated once in remap_3d_create_plan (plan->d_sendbuf at lines 574 and 758, plan->d_scratch at lines 583 and 848), so nothing forces the per-call re-creation. The cost is real: FFT3dKokkos issues up to four remaps per 3d FFT and PPPMKokkos does several FFTs per timestep. Two mitigating notes: on a host-only Kokkos build create_mirror_view returns the source view without allocating, so this only costs on GPU builds without GPU-aware MPI; and when plan->memory == 0 the mirrored view is the caller-supplied d_buf (line 114), so a cached mirror must be size-checked rather than blindly reused.

fix:
Create plan->h_sendbuf once in remap_3d_create_plan right after plan->d_sendbuf is allocated (both the point-to-point path at line 574 and the collective path at line 758), and likewise plan->h_scratch after plan->d_scratch (lines 583/848). In remap_3d_kokkos, replace the unconditional creation with a size-guarded refresh, e.g.
  if (!plan->usegpu_aware) {
    if (plan->h_scratch.extent(0) < d_scratch.extent(0))
      plan->h_scratch = Kokkos::create_mirror_view(d_scratch);
    if (plan->h_sendbuf.extent(0) < plan->d_sendbuf.extent(0))
      plan->h_sendbuf = Kokkos::create_mirror_view(plan->d_sendbuf);
    ...
  }
so the buffers are reallocated only when they need to grow. Take care that the subview deep_copies at lines 167-168 and the full deep_copies at 209/244/262 still use matching ranges rather than the whole (possibly larger) cached view.

reasoning:
ImproperUmbrellaKokkos<DeviceType>::compute() contains, inside the per-timestep body under the comment `// zero warning flag`:
  k_warning_flag = DAT::tdual_int_scalar("ImproperUmbrella::warning_flag");
  d_warning_flag = k_warning_flag.template view<DeviceType>();
  h_warning_flag = k_warning_flag.view_host();
  h_warning_flag() = 0;
(lines 98-101). That is a fresh DualView allocation (host + device scalar) plus two view rebinds every timestep, where only the `h_warning_flag() = 0` is actually needed. Every KOKKOS dihedral style does it the right way: e.g. DihedralCharmmKokkos allocates it once in the constructor (dihedral_charmm_kokkos.cpp:50-52) and its compute() only does `h_warning_flag() = 0; k_warning_flag.modify_host(); k_warning_flag.template sync<DeviceType>();` (lines 118-120), the same three lines that already follow the allocation here. Grepping `tdual_int_scalar("` across src/KOKKOS shows exactly two styles that allocate it inside compute(): improper_umbrella_kokkos.cpp:98 and improper_fourier_kokkos.cpp:100 -- everything else does it in the constructor.

fix:
Move the three lines
  k_warning_flag = DAT::tdual_int_scalar("ImproperUmbrella::warning_flag");
  d_warning_flag = k_warning_flag.template view<DeviceType>();
  h_warning_flag = k_warning_flag.view_host();
from compute() into the ImproperUmbrellaKokkos constructor (matching dihedral_charmm_kokkos.cpp:50-52), leaving only `h_warning_flag() = 0; k_warning_flag.modify_host(); k_warning_flag.template sync<DeviceType>();` in compute(). Apply the identical change to ImproperFourierKokkos (src/KOKKOS/improper_fourier_kokkos.cpp:100-102).
